feat(shim): present a repository-scoped credential to the lifecycle listener (#266)

#264 put a repository-scoped credential in front of the six identity-lifecycle
tools. Two callers reach that listener and neither presented one: measured,
`grep -c Authorization` returned 0 in both, while the deployment already ran
with the listener split on. Deploying the check as it stood would have answered
every registration with 401 — no agent could register, and so nothing could be
signed either. This is the arrangement that makes #264 shippable.

WHO MINTS. Each caller, for itself, by running the shipped
`innsegl admin-credential mint` inside a throwaway container that mounts the
deployment's private signing key read-only, has no network, no writable root
filesystem and no privilege escalation, and exits. `--user 0:0` because the
one-shot leaves that key 0400 and root-owned, which is the point: the image's
own 1000:1000 cannot read it, so neither could a compromised innsegl-mcp, which
does not mount the volume at all. Nothing copies the key onto the host and
neither script ever sees it; the only value that crosses is the credential, on
a pipe. A long-lived minter would be a standing mint oracle for anything that
could reach it — a container that lives 0.23s is reachable only through the
container runtime's API, which already carried the privilege to mount that
volume.

HOW OFTEN: never, unless the listener refuses. The first attempt of a process
carries no credential, and a 401 — nothing else — mints one and repeats the
same call exactly once. So a deployment that enforces nothing makes no 401,
mints nothing, and behaves byte-for-byte as before. Single-listener mode is
unchanged by construction rather than by a mode someone has to set correctly,
and there is no probe, no health read and no configuration to get wrong.

WHAT EXPIRY DOES: nothing special, which is the point. The credential lives
fifteen minutes and cannot be withdrawn, because a revocation list would be a
dependency on the issuer being reachable. A run that registers, signs and
retires can outlive the one it started with, so each call that is refused mints
a fresh credential and repeats itself. No clock is read in either script and no
`exp` is parsed: the server's own refusal is the only trigger, so there is
never a second opinion about when a credential died.

WHERE IT IS KEPT: one shell variable, for one process. Not exported, so no
child inherits it; not written, so no marker, pointer or file holds it; not
logged; and never in an argument vector — `ps` publishes the arguments of every
process on this machine, and a bearer token there is replayable for the rest of
its life. It reaches the shim's client on stdin and curl through a `-K`
configuration on a pipe, which is also why it is not a here-document: most
shells implement one as a temporary file.

AND A REFUSAL SAYS WHAT TO DO. The listener answers every credential failure
with one byte-identical sentence by design, because a distinguishable reason is
an oracle — so the caller is the only place an operator can be told which
command mints and against which key. A 401 with no remedy strands an agent
holding finished work, which is the failure this exists to prevent.

A STOP STILL NEVER BLOCKS. A stop that cannot authenticate warns and exits 0
like every other stop failure; the run expires on its TTL, which is what the
reaper is for. A blocked stop was measured producing nine repeated invocations.

The shim reads origin's URL for one purpose and one only: a credential has to
name a repository BEFORE the call it authorises, and the only tool that could
answer that is behind the credential. Nothing else about E11 moved — no repo,
branch, task or worktree reaches a tool argument from the shim, which case 14
now asserts against every call the suite makes.

Drove it: OPS-069 (a credential is presented, scoped to the repository, and
only after a refusal), OPS-070 (one spent mid-run is re-minted per refused call
rather than failing the caller), OPS-071 (the value reaches no marker, no file,
no log line, no export and no argument vector), OPS-072 (a stop that cannot
authenticate exits 0, and the refusal names the mint command and the key),
OPS-073 (with nothing to authenticate, no request carries a credential and
nothing is minted, over every case in both suites). OPS-056..068 were taken
across other branches, so the block starts at the measured next free id.

Proved against the running deployment with the shipped verifier in front of its
admin listener: an unauthenticated handshake is refused 401, and a full cycle of
session start, subagent registration, tool call, signature and retirement
completes behind it.

